Title: EP01 — 스펙이 뭔가?
Credit: Written by
Author: vibecode.town
Series: Vibecode Town Season 1
Source: Act 1-1 (I thought it worked) + Act 1-2 (Monday morning, everything broke)
Draft date: 2026-03-18
Duration: 3:30-4:00
Notes: v3 (6-segment format, narrator-only, 2D flat vector, Vee solo)

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00-0:15]                                       */
/* emotion_curve: EXCITEMENT → SHOCK                                  */
/* narrative_stage: HOOK                                               */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */
/* shorts_candidate */

= Five monitors flash green "BUILD SUCCESS" → cascade into red errors.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT

/* vee_reaction: 1/6 */

= Five monitors glow in a dark room. Vee throws both arms up. Every screen flashes green: "BUILD SUCCESS."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You asked AI to build it. It said yes.

= One screen turns red. Then two. Then all five. Error messages cascade.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three seconds later, nothing works. And you can't read a single error.

> TITLE CARD: **스펙이 뭔가?**

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 2: MISCONCEPTION [0:15-0:45]                               */
/* emotion_curve: OVERCONFIDENCE → CONFUSION                          */
/* narrative_stage: MISCONCEPTION                                      */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

= Vee's false assumption: "AI가 만드니까 스펙 필요 없지?"

INT. VEE'S ROOM - DAY (3 DAYS EARLIER)

> INSERT: "3일 전"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three days ago, Vee discovered vibe coding. Type what you want. AI builds it. Done.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: screen */

= MONTAGE: Vee types → app appears. Types → app appears. Each time faster.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A to-do app. A database. A login page. Five minutes each.

/* vee_reaction: 2/6 */

= Vee spins in her chair, arms crossed, nodding. Five terminal windows run simultaneously.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
By day three, she had five agents running. She couldn't explain what any of them did. But they ran. So she assumed they worked.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why write a spec? AI handles everything. Right?

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 3: THE_CRACK [0:45-1:15]                                   */
/* emotion_curve: CONFUSION → DOUBT                                   */
/* narrative_stage: THE_CRACK                                          */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

= "코드가 돌아갔다. 근데 뭘 하는지 설명 못 한다."

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT (PRESENT)

= Vee scrolls through code. It doesn't end. She scrolls faster. Still doesn't end.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ten thousand, eight hundred and forty-seven lines. Almost half of it — duplicated.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= Counter ticks: 10,847. Splits into 6,647 (white) and 4,200 (red, peels away).

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Five AI sessions built the same functions five different ways. None of them knew the others existed.

/* vee_reaction: 3/6 */

= Vee stares at the screen. She can explain what she typed. She can't explain what it built.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The code ran. But she couldn't explain what it does. That's not a bug. That's a different kind of broken.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 4: CORE [1:15-3:00]                                        */
/* emotion_curve: DOUBT → CLARITY                                     */
/* narrative_stage: CORE                                               */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */
/* extended_metaphor_start */

= Building metaphor: blueprint vs. random stacking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's a building. Built with a blueprint. Foundation first, walls on top, roof last. Everything connects because someone decided the order before the first brick.

= A building rises on screen, piece by piece, following a glowing blue blueprint.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now here's a building without a blueprint. Just... stack things. Whatever arrives first goes at the bottom.

= Blocks pile randomly. The structure wobbles. Leans.

/* shorts_candidate */

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It looks like a building. It stands — for now. But the plumbing runs through the attic, and the front door opens into a wall.

= The random building collapses. Blocks tumble.

/* extended_metaphor_end */

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's what happened to Vee's code. Each AI agent stacked features without knowing the plan. Because there was no plan.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= Text overlay: "66% of IT projects fail — #1 cause: incomplete requirements" (Standish CHAOS)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Industry data backs this up. Two-thirds of software projects fail. The number one reason? Nobody wrote down what they're building.

= Bar chart animates: $1 → $10 → $100.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A bug caught during planning costs one dollar. The same bug in production? A hundred. Barry Boehm proved this in 1981.

= Blank page appears. Four boxes draw themselves:

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So what IS a spec? Not a hundred-page document. Four questions.

= Text fills each box: "What am I trying to do?" / "Why doesn't the current way work?" / "How should it work?" / "What tools will I use?"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Purpose. Reason. Method. Means. Answer those four, and you have a spec. That's it.

/* vee_reaction: 4/6 */

= Vee pulls out paper and pencil. Turns her back to the monitors.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Vee stopped coding. She started writing what was bothering her.

= CU: Paper. Messy handwriting: "I don't know what I built."

= The pencil stops. Then moves again: "I want to use my home computer from anywhere."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One sentence that explains everything. Not features. Not tools. The reason.

/* shorts_candidate */

= Below: "Files should stay in sync." "Access from any device." "Can't drop the connection."

= CU: Security check result — "Line 847: shell injection."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now the errors make sense. Line eight forty-seven wasn't random. It was an open door she never meant to build.

/* vee_reaction: 5/6 */

= Vee picks up the paper. Turns back to the monitors. One error line highlights: "Connection refused: sync agent cannot reach host."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Same errors. But now, one line makes sense. Because she knows what she's looking for.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 5: REFRAME [3:00-3:30]                                     */
/* emotion_curve: CLARITY → UNDERSTANDING                             */
/* narrative_stage: REFRAME                                            */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= Zoom out: bigger picture.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's what most people miss. This isn't about Cursor, or ChatGPT, or Claude. Every AI tool wants the same thing from you.

= Icons of AI tools appear, then fade. A single question mark remains.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A definition of "done." What does finished look like? If you can't answer that, no amount of AI will save you.

= The four spec boxes reappear, small, connected to a larger system diagram.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A spec isn't documentation. It's the shortest path between frustration and something that actually works.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 6: OUTRO_CTA [3:30-3:55]                                   */
/* emotion_curve: UNDERSTANDING → CONFIDENCE                          */
/* narrative_stage: OUTRO_CTA                                          */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

= Mirror of HOOK. Same room, different person.

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

= Same composition as HOOK. Five monitors. Red errors. Dark room.

= Vee pins a piece of paper next to the monitor: "I want to use my home computer from anywhere."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Everything is still broken. But now she knows what to fix.

/* vee_reaction: 6/6 */

= Vee's expression: determined. Slight nod. Cracks her knuckles.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Next time — she wrote the spec. And AI gave her something completely different from what she expected. Again.

> END CARD: 구독 + EP02 "왼팔이 28개" 티저
